/** \page news News

@htmlinclude sidebar.html

\htmlonly

    <div id="content">

        <table cellspacing="0" class="news-article">
            <tr>
                <th class="news-article-header" >
                    <b>TR-4 released</b>
                    <br/>
                    <i>Posted on June 6, 2010</i>
                </th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="news-article-body">
                    The fourth technical release includes not only a complete
                    review of the systems programming module (Pt-System) and the 
                    networking module (Pt-Net), but also two new modules: Pt-Http
                    and Pt-XmlRpc. The logging facilities have been integrated into the
                    Pt-System module and the text handling classes have been inegrated
                    into the Pt core module. This leads to reduced binary size and fewer
                    shared libraries.

                    At least the following modules will be part of the 1.0 release:
                    Pt (core), Pt-System, Pt-Net, Pt-Unit, Pt-Xml.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <br/>

        <table cellspacing="0" class="news-article">
            <tr>
                <th class="news-article-header" >
                    <b>TR-3 released</b>
                    <br/>
                    <i>Posted on February 15, 2008</i>
                </th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="news-article-body">
                    Starting with this TR, the Pt core module is in a soft-freeze,
                    thus source compatibility should not be broken except in case of
                    critical bugs. Changes and additions can still be made of course,
                    if they do not break sorce compatibility.
                    New features include support for Solaris, and OS-X. The GUI module
                    for Mac OS-X uses the Objective-C Cocoa API's, though it is not yet
                    complete.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <br/>

        <table cellspacing="0" class="news-article">
            <tr>
                <th class="news-article-header" >
                    <b>TR-2 released</b>
                    <br/>
                    <i>Posted on October 16, 2007</i>
                </th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="news-article-body">
                    The second technical release adds support for async I/O and
                    I/O multiplexing on all available I/O devices, atomic
                    operations for integers and pointers, a logging framework, date
                    and time classes, a new module for reflection and updates to the
                    Unit testing framework.
                    AIX unix is supported now and Pt has been shown to compile and 
                    run successfully on symbian based operating systems.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <br/>

        <table cellspacing="0" class="news-article">
            <tr>
                <th class="news-article-header" >
                    <b>New Project Website</b>
                    <br/>
                    <i>Posted on February 25, 2007</i>
                </th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="news-article-body">
                    The new project website integrates the generated doxygen
                    documentation into the project pages. The online reference
                    documentation was improved and contains class groups for easier
                    navigation.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <br/>

        <table cellspacing="0" class="news-article">
            <tr>
                <th class="news-article-header" >
                    <b>TR-1 released</b>
                    <br/>
                    <i>Posted on February 5, 2007</i>
                </th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="news-article-body">
                    This slightly longer development sprint has lead to the TR-1
                    release. Many improvements have been made to the core module
                    and the Gfx module. A new module for network programming was
                    added (%Pt::Net). Pt can now be built wit the intel compiler
                    on win32. The code is available as a tarball in the download
                    section or as a tag in the subversion repository (tags/tr1).
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <br/>

        <table cellspacing="0" class="news-article">
            <tr>
                <th class="news-article-header" >
                    <b>Development News</b>
                    <br/>
                    <i>Posted on February 5, 2007</i>
                </th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="news-article-body">
                    During the last month, enhancements have been made to the
                    Gfx module and the core module, Most notably to the image
                    classes and the reflection API. A new module for unit testing
                    was added. Pt::Unit allows protocol- and data-driven testing.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <br/>

        <br/>

        <table cellspacing="0" class="news-article">
            <tr>
                <th class="news-article-header" >
                    <b>Pt and Tntnet at LinuxWorldExpo in Cologne</b>
                    <br/>
                    <i>Posted on October 14, 2006</i>
                </th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="news-article-body">
                    Platinum was presented together with Tntnet, the open-source
                    web application server, at LinuxWorldExpo in Cologne in the
                    open source pavillion.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <br/>

        <table cellspacing="0" class="news-article">
            <tr>
                <th class="news-article-header" >
                    <b>Pt on Sourceforge</b>
                    <br/>
                    <i>Posted on September 22, 2006</i>
                </th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="news-article-body">
                    The Platinum C++ framework is now hosted on sourceforge.net.
                    The source code is available through an SVN repository and
                    generated documentation can be viewed online now.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <br/>
    </div>

\endhtmlonly

